  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nRelatively quiet weather conditions will continue through \nSaturday as western CONUS troughing edges eastward into an \nexpansive northeastern CONUS ridge. This will allow for \npersistent WAA within strong southwesterly low-level flow. \nGiven 925 hPa temps within 1-2C of record values for late \nNovember combined with limited cloud cover, near-record or \nrecord high temperatures are expected today and Saturday. \nToday's record high of 68F (69F) at Chicago (Rockford) is in \njeopardy. In fact, some locations may reach 70F. With regards to\na chance at 70F today, there have been only 4 occurrences (1 \noccurrence) on record of 70F or higher at Chicago (Rockford) \nthis late in the season.\n\nA mid-level wave currently approaching the northern California \ncoast will shift across the Four Corners region on Saturday \nbefore becoming increasingly sheared while crossing the Upper \nMississippi River Valley on Sunday. Upper-level jet support will\nfocus across central Iowa into Minnesota Saturday night before \nweakening and lifting well north of the area, so the magnitude \nof deep-layer forcing remains questionable. However, PWAT \nvalues up to 1.2 inches (solidly above 90th percentile) and an \nalignment of the sheared mid-level wave and surface cold front \nsupport a broken to solid band of rain drifting eastward across\nthe CWA on Sunday. Though mid-level lapse rates will be \nmarginal at best, would not be surprised to see sporadic \nlightning as close as the south half of Illinois.\n\nBehind Sunday's cold front, a substantially colder and drier \nairmass (PWATs under 0.3 inches) will build in across the region\nnext week. Temperatures will run seasonably cold for late \nNovember. Fairly low inversion heights will limit the potential \nfor lake effect precip within north to northeast flow Monday \nnight into Wednesday, though cannot rule out some LES flurries \nduring this time. Will also need to monitor the evolution of a \ndeveloping Gulf low midweek as it tracks somewhere between the \nOhio Valley and eastern seaboard. Outlying ensemble members \nsuggest the system could clip our area with precip (primarily \nsnow) on Wednesday, though precip will have to contend with the \nantecedent very dry airmass.\n\nKluber",
